  "query_preview": "..\nSYNOPTIC SETUP:\nAn low pressure system moving south into northern California cut\noff by the northern stream will continue to lift northeast over\nthe next several days. Ahead of it, a ridge will depart the\nnorthern High Plains early this morning for North Dakota and \nsoutheast low level flow will continue with breezy conditions.\n\nAs with the last round of ensemble data, the latest guidance is a\nbit further south with the storm track and shows a tight gradient\nin precipitation amounts over our northern CWA. Although, any \nembedded convective elements that concentrate may raise totals \nlocally. The NBM generally gives 0.5\" to 1.5\" of total QPF with \nthe heaviest corridor south of the Missouri River and west of the \nYellowstone River. Lesser rainfall amounts are expected toward the\nCanadian border and MT/ND border. Look for showers to become most\nwidespread Friday night and Saturday before the system slowly\nexits Sunday. The Weather Prediction Center has highlighted a \nmarginal risk of excessive rainfall for late today through \nSaturday.\n\nUpper ridging will yield drier and warmer conditions for early\nnext week. Those conditions then look to hold steady through the\nend of the work week. \n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\nMeanwhile, confidence is below average for this upcoming \nprecipitation event for Friday into Saturday given poor run to run\ncontinuity among the guidance, a trend toward the south, and a \ntight gradient over NE Montana. To that end, widespread rainfall \nranging from 0.25\" in the north to 1.00\" in the south and \nsouthwest are still largely most likely, with the highest totals \nsouth of the Missouri River and west of the Yellowstone River, \nconcentrated over SW Phillips, Petroleum, and Garfield Counties.\n\n/GAH",
